    "physical_description": "Cotton batik sarong with applied gold dust over-design (kain prada). The body of the cloth (badan)  has a flowering plant design with nutmegs, lotuses, peonies, carnations and other flowers, and with perching birds and butterflies. The head (kepala) has two panels with flowers and Chinese phoenix designs flanking a double row of triangular motifs (tumpal), a design structure which derives from Indian printed cotton trade cloths.",
    "summary": "This type of sarong made of gold dusted batik cloth (or <i>kain prada</i>) was a luxury cloth used for weddings in the north coast of Java  by Chinese and Javanese, and usually worn on only one occasion. Textiles of this quality are now extremely rare, though other types of <i>prada</i> are still occasionally made. The flowering plant design, like many of the designs of batik cloths made in the north coast of Java, shows a fascinating blend of influences--Chinese, Persian, Indian and indigenous Javanese.",
